---
import type { NovaGroup } from '../../pipeline/lib/types.ts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import NovaBadge from '../components/NovaBadge.astro';
import { href, markerByTag, markerHref } from '../lib/data.ts';
import { NOVA } from '../lib/nova.ts';

const link = (tag: string, text: string) => {
  const m = markerByTag.get(tag);
  return m ? `<a href="${markerHref(m)}">${text}</a>` : text;
};
---

<Base
  title="What is NOVA? The four food processing groups explained"
  description="NOVA classifies food by how much and why it is processed: unprocessed, culinary ingredients, processed, and ultra-processed. How it works, how ultra-processed foods are identified, and its limits."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>What is NOVA?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      NOVA is a way of classifying food by the extent and purpose of the industrial processing it has been through,
      rather than by the nutrients it contains. It sorts every food and drink into one of four groups.
    </p>

    <p>
      It was developed by researchers at the Center for Epidemiological Studies in Health and Nutrition (NUPENS) at the
      University of São Paulo, led by Carlos Monteiro, and first published in 2009. "Processing" here means the
      physical, biological and chemical methods applied to food after it is separated from nature and before it is eaten
      or cooked. NOVA is the classification behind most research on <em>ultra-processed food</em>, and Brazil's official
      dietary guidelines are built on it.
    </p>

    <h2>The four groups</h2>
    {
      ([1, 2, 3, 4] as NovaGroup[]).map((g) => (
        <section class="group">
          <h3>
            <NovaBadge group={g} /> Group {g}: {NOVA[g].name}
          </h3>
          <p>{NOVA[g].definition}</p>
          <p class="meta">Examples: {NOVA[g].examples}.</p>
        </section>
      ))
    }

    <h2>How ultra-processed foods are identified</h2>
    <p>
      The defining feature of a group 4 product is not that it is made in a factory, but that it is formulated from
      substances that a home cook would not have. NOVA's authors describe two kinds of tell-tale ingredient, which they
      call <dfn>markers</dfn> of ultra-processing:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Substances of no or rare culinary use.</strong> These are extracted from foods or derived from them:
          sugars such as <Fragment set:html={link('en:fructose', 'fructose')} />, <Fragment set:html={link('en:high-fructose-corn-syrup', 'high-fructose corn syrup')} />,
          <Fragment set:html={link('en:invert-sugar', 'invert sugar')} />, <Fragment set:html={link('en:maltodextrin', 'maltodextrin')} /> and
          <Fragment set:html={link('en:dextrose', 'dextrose')} />; modified oils such as hydrogenated or interesterified oils; and protein
          sources such as hydrolysed proteins, soy protein isolate, <Fragment set:html={link('en:gluten', 'gluten')} />, casein and
          <Fragment set:html={link('en:whey', 'whey')} /> protein.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>"Cosmetic" additives.</strong> These are additives whose job is to make the final product palatable or
          appealing rather than to preserve it: <Fragment set:html={link('en:flavouring', 'flavourings')} />, flavour enhancers,
          <Fragment set:html={link('en:colour', 'colours')} />, <Fragment set:html={link('en:emulsifier', 'emulsifiers')} />, sweeteners,
          thickeners, and anti-foaming, bulking, carbonating, foaming, gelling and glazing agents.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      One marker is enough. Plain rolled oats are group 1. Oats baked with sugar and oil into a granola are group 3. Add
      flavouring or an emulsifier to that granola and it becomes group 4, even though it looks and tastes much the same.
    </p>

    <h2>How this site gets its NOVA groups</h2>
    <p>
      We do not classify products ourselves. Every group on this site comes from
      <a href="https://world.openfoodfacts.org/nova">Open Food Facts</a>, which computes it automatically from the ingredient
      list, additives and category that contributors enter. Their implementation turns the NOVA definitions into lists of
      ingredients, additives and categories, each tagged with the minimum group it implies; a product gets the highest
      group any of its markers points to. On every product page we show which markers were found and highlight them in
      the ingredient list.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits worth knowing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>It is an estimate from crowdsourced data.</strong> If an ingredient list is incomplete, mistyped or out
          of date, the group can be wrong. Products whose recipe changed may still show the old one.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Processing is not the same as nutrition.</strong> Table sugar and butter are minimally processed
          ingredients; some ultra-processed products, such as certain wholegrain breads, have a reasonable nutrient
          profile. NOVA answers "how and why was this processed?", which is a different question from "is this
          healthy?".
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>The research is about patterns, not single products.</strong> Studies linking ultra-processed food to
          poorer health outcomes look at the share of a whole diet that is group 4. Researchers still debate why
          that link exists, and how much of it comes from processing itself rather than from the nutrients and eating
          patterns that come with it.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Group 4 is broad.</strong> A soft drink and a mass-produced wholemeal loaf with an emulsifier are both
          group 4. That is why each product page lists the specific markers, so you can judge them yourself.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Further reading</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Monteiro CA, Cannon G, Levy RB, et al. "Ultra-processed foods: what they are and how to identify them."
          <cite>Public Health Nutrition</cite> 22(5), 2019, pages 936 to 941.
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S1368980018003762">doi:10.1017/S1368980018003762</a>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Open Food Facts, <a href="https://world.openfoodfacts.org/nova">"NOVA groups for food processing"</a>, which
          explains their implementation.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={href()}>Look up a product</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .group h3 {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
  dfn {
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
</style>
